private static void permuteHelper(int[] nums, int start, List<List<Integer>> result) {
    if (start == nums.length) {
        List<Integer> permutation = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int num : nums) {
            permutation.add(num);
        }
        result.add(permutation);
        return;
    }
    for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
        swap(nums, start, i);
        permuteHelper(nums, start + 1, result);
        swap(nums, start, i);
    }
}

private static void swap(int[] nums, int i, int j) {
    int temp = nums[i];
    nums[i] = nums[j];
    nums[j] = temp;
}
```
This code provides an implementation of the standard backtracking algorithm for solving the permutations problem, where the `permute` function takes an integer array as input and returns a list of lists of integers representing all possible permutations of the array. The `permuteHelper` function is a helper function that does the actual work of generating all permutations, with the `start` parameter indicating the position of the current permutation and the `result` parameter storing the final list of permutations. The `swap` function is a helper function that swaps the values at two indices in the input array.

```
public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
    List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
    permute(nums, 0, result);
    return result;
}

private void permute(int[] nums, int start, List<List<Integer>> result) {
    if (start == nums.length) {
        result.add(Arrays.stream(nums).boxed().collect(Collectors.toList()));
        return;
    }

    for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
        swap(nums, start, i);
        permute(nums, start + 1, result);
        swap(nums, start, i);
    }
}

private void swap(int[] nums, int i, int j) {
    int temp = nums[i];
    nums[i] = nums[j];
    nums[j] = temp;
}
```
This code uses thebacktracking algorithm to generate all possible permutations of the array. The `permute` method takes three parameters: `nums`, the array to permute, `start`, the starting index of the permutation, and `result`, the list of permutations. The `swap` method swaps the values at two indices in the array.

The code begins by checking if the permutation is complete, that is, if the next index is equal to the length of the array. If it is, the permutation is added to the result. If not, the method calls itself with the next index and the same result list.

For example, when `start` is 0, the method will permute the values at indices 0, 1, and 2, then swap them back to their original positions and continue permuting the remaining values. The permutations are added to the result list. The same process is repeated until all possible permutations have been generated.

The `swap` method is called to swap the values at the starting index and the index of the next permutation. This results in the values at the starting index and the next index being swapped, such that the value at the starting index is moved to the next index. The value at the original next index is moved to the starting index, and the values between the starting index and the new next index are swapped in reverse order. This results in all possible permutations of the array.

